1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:01,337
Take a flat sheet of paper.

2
00:00:01,607 --> 00:00:05,891
You can roll it into a cylinder without stretching it,
and without a single crease.

3
00:00:06,450 --> 00:00:09,852
Now bend the cylinder around,
and glue its two ends together.

4
00:00:10,122 --> 00:00:12,316
You get a torus, the surface of a donut.

5
00:00:12,883 --> 00:00:14,142
Here is a harder question.

6
00:00:14,412 --> 00:00:18,839
Can you lay a torus-shaped membrane smoothly
over a sphere, without any creases?

7
00:00:19,109 --> 00:00:19,729
You cannot.

8
00:00:19,999 --> 00:00:22,873
Somewhere, the membrane has
to fold back on itself.

9
00:00:23,433 --> 00:00:25,856
Electrons in a crystal do exactly this.

10
00:00:26,126 --> 00:00:30,166
In a two-band crystal,
every band presses a torus onto a sphere

11
00:00:30,186 --> 00:00:34,419
and hidden in the folds is a topological number,
the Chern number.

12
00:00:34,983 --> 00:00:40,098
This video follows three papers by Shin-Ming Huang,
which read all of this from a

13
00:00:40,118 --> 00:00:45,359
single object, the projector onto the occupied states,
without ever fixing a phase.

14
00:00:46,717 --> 00:00:47,584
Here is the stage.

15
00:00:47,854 --> 00:00:51,518
On the left is the Brillouin zone,
a square of crystal momenta.

16
00:00:51,788 --> 00:00:54,132
Opposite edges describe the same states

17
00:00:54,152 --> 00:00:57,830
so they are glued together,
and the square is really a torus.

18
00:00:58,384 --> 00:01:00,045
On the right is the space of states.

19
00:01:00,315 --> 00:01:04,128
For two bands, a state up to
its overall phase is fixed by

20
00:01:04,148 --> 00:01:08,159
one unit vector, the expectation
value of the Pauli matrices.

21
00:01:08,429 --> 00:01:10,376
That vector is a point on the Bloch sphere.

22
00:01:10,934 --> 00:01:11,850
So what is the map?

23
00:01:12,120 --> 00:01:17,248
A two-band Hamiltonian is a vector d of k,
dotted into the Pauli matrices.

24
00:01:17,518 --> 00:01:20,793
Its upper band is the state whose
Bloch vector points along d.

25
00:01:21,063 --> 00:01:26,443
So the map is simply this: k goes to n of k,
the vector d divided by its length.

26
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:29,834
We use one model throughout,
the Qi Wu Zhang model.

27
00:01:30,104 --> 00:01:36,482
Its vector d is sine k x, sine k y,
and m minus cosine k x minus cosine k y.

28
00:01:36,752 --> 00:01:40,608
At Gamma, d points straight down,
so Gamma lands on the south pole.

29
00:01:40,878 --> 00:01:45,039
At X and at M, d points straight up,
so both land on the north pole.

30
00:01:45,600 --> 00:01:47,318
The mass m sets the topology.

31
00:01:47,588 --> 00:01:50,232
For m between zero and two, the image wraps

32
00:01:50,252 --> 00:01:53,082
the whole sphere,
and the Chern number is one.

33
00:01:53,352 --> 00:01:56,145
That is the band we follow,
with m equal to one.

34
00:01:56,415 --> 00:02:00,107
For m above two,
the z component of d is always positive

35
00:02:00,127 --> 00:02:04,616
every state stays near the north pole,
and the Chern number is zero.

36
00:02:04,886 --> 00:02:08,032
Our trivial example is m
equal to three point two.

37
00:02:08,600 --> 00:02:11,400
Now paint the whole grid onto
the sphere, cell by cell.

38
00:02:11,670 --> 00:02:13,797
Walk around a small cell counterclockwise.

39
00:02:14,067 --> 00:02:18,793
Its image is walked either the same way
as most of the map, which we call face

40
00:02:18,813 --> 00:02:23,297
up and color blue, or the mirror way,
which we call flipped and color red.

41
00:02:23,567 --> 00:02:27,434
The flipped cells surround the point M,
where both momenta equal pi.

42
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:29,972
Where blue meets red, the map folds.

43
00:02:30,242 --> 00:02:31,434
Keep this picture in mind.

44
00:02:31,704 --> 00:02:35,165
We will come back to it again and again,
adding one layer at a time.

45
00:02:36,533 --> 00:02:38,964
The first paper, by Huang and Giataganas

46
00:02:38,984 --> 00:02:42,639
starts with a simple question:
how far apart are two states?

47
00:02:43,200 --> 00:02:47,758
A state does not care about its overall phase,
so compare projectors instead.

48
00:02:48,028 --> 00:02:52,817
Two states define an angle theta:
its cosine is the size of their overlap.

49
00:02:53,383 --> 00:02:57,794
On the Bloch sphere, the two Bloch vectors
make an angle of two theta at the center.

50
00:02:58,064 --> 00:03:02,762
Orthogonal states, with theta equal to
ninety degrees, sit at opposite points.

51
00:03:03,316 --> 00:03:06,978
Measured with the quantum metric,
this sphere has radius one half.

52
00:03:07,248 --> 00:03:10,178
So the arc between the two
states has length theta

53
00:03:10,198 --> 00:03:12,183
that is the Fubini Study distance.

54
00:03:12,453 --> 00:03:14,803
And the straight chord has length sine theta

55
00:03:14,823 --> 00:03:18,035
that is the Hilbert Schmidt
distance between the projectors.

56
00:03:18,305 --> 00:03:19,787
For nearby states the two agree.

57
00:03:20,350 --> 00:03:21,894
Now let two bands be occupied.

58
00:03:22,164 --> 00:03:26,180
At one momentum, the occupied states
span a two-dimensional subspace.

59
00:03:26,450 --> 00:03:28,396
Think of it as a plane through the origin.

60
00:03:28,666 --> 00:03:31,085
At another momentum,
they span another plane.

61
00:03:31,650 --> 00:03:34,022
Two planes are compared by principal angles.

62
00:03:34,292 --> 00:03:36,233
Picture the floor and a half-open door.

63
00:03:36,503 --> 00:03:40,311
Along the hinge, the planes share a direction,
so that angle is zero.

64
00:03:40,581 --> 00:03:43,341
Across the hinge,
they differ by the opening angle.

65
00:03:43,900 --> 00:03:47,988
In general, the singular value
decomposition of the overlap matrix

66
00:03:48,008 --> 00:03:52,593
pairs each direction u in the first
plane with a direction v in the second

67
00:03:52,613 --> 00:03:56,326
and the principal angles are
the angles between these pairs.

68
00:03:56,596 --> 00:03:59,679
They do not depend on the
basis chosen in either plane

69
00:03:59,699 --> 00:04:03,987
and the squared Hilbert Schmidt distance
is the sum of their squared sines.

70
00:04:05,350 --> 00:04:08,797
Now sample the zone at four
hundred and eighty four momenta

71
00:04:08,817 --> 00:04:12,616
and for every pair,
write down the distance between their states.

72
00:04:12,886 --> 00:04:16,785
Careful: this is not the length of
the yellow line in momentum space.

73
00:04:17,055 --> 00:04:19,364
It is the chord between the
two states on the sphere.

74
00:04:19,933 --> 00:04:21,663
Here is the whole table as an image.

75
00:04:21,933 --> 00:04:24,781
Row i and column j show the distance D i j

76
00:04:24,801 --> 00:04:29,014
black means the same state,
and white means orthogonal states.

77
00:04:29,284 --> 00:04:31,773
The diagonal is black,
and the stripes come from

78
00:04:31,793 --> 00:04:34,073
the order in which the momenta are numbered.

79
00:04:34,633 --> 00:04:37,916
Can you rebuild the shape of the
states from this table alone

80
00:04:37,936 --> 00:04:40,244
without the momenta and without the states?

81
00:04:40,514 --> 00:04:43,827
Because the Hilbert Schmidt
distance is a straight-line

82
00:04:43,847 --> 00:04:46,554
distance between matrices,
the answer is yes.

83
00:04:46,824 --> 00:04:49,666
Multidimensional scaling looks for points in

84
00:04:49,686 --> 00:04:52,723
ordinary space whose
distances match the table.

85
00:04:53,283 --> 00:04:54,001
Watch it work.

86
00:04:54,271 --> 00:04:57,637
Start from a random cloud,
and let every pair of points pull

87
00:04:57,657 --> 00:05:00,910
or push like a spring,
until its length matches the table.

88
00:05:01,180 --> 00:05:03,921
Each frame here is one real
step of the algorithm.

89
00:05:04,483 --> 00:05:07,818
For the Chern band,
the points settle onto a complete

90
00:05:07,838 --> 00:05:11,299
sphere of radius one half,
with an empty cavity inside.

91
00:05:11,569 --> 00:05:15,941
It is the Bloch sphere,
rebuilt from distances alone, up to a rotation.

92
00:05:16,500 --> 00:05:22,298
For the trivial band, with m equal to three point two,
every state lies near the north pole

93
00:05:22,318 --> 00:05:26,710
so the table only describes a small cap,
and that is what comes back.

94
00:05:26,980 --> 00:05:29,475
The cap is fully covered,
but it never closes.

95
00:05:29,745 --> 00:05:34,388
A closed surface or an open patch:
that is a first glimpse of the topology.

96
00:05:34,950 --> 00:05:36,793
But the table has two blind spots.

97
00:05:37,063 --> 00:05:40,715
A shape and its mirror image
have exactly the same distances

98
00:05:40,735 --> 00:05:43,530
so the table cannot tell
face up from flipped.

99
00:05:43,800 --> 00:05:46,763
And three layers lying on top
of each other look like one.

100
00:05:47,317 --> 00:05:50,924
The Chern number counts how many
times the sphere is covered

101
00:05:50,944 --> 00:05:53,281
with signs, so we need the orientation.

102
00:05:53,551 --> 00:05:57,626
Orientation is local:
we have to zoom in on the derivative of the map.

103
00:05:58,983 --> 00:06:00,165
Zoom in on one momentum.

104
00:06:00,435 --> 00:06:04,761
A tiny circle of momenta around it
lands on the sphere as a tiny ellipse.

105
00:06:05,316 --> 00:06:07,174
That ellipse is the quantum metric.

106
00:06:07,444 --> 00:06:11,463
A small step delta k moves
the state by a distance d s

107
00:06:11,483 --> 00:06:15,277
with d s squared equal to
g i j delta k i delta k j

108
00:06:15,297 --> 00:06:19,988
where g is one quarter of the
derivatives of n dotted together.

109
00:06:20,550 --> 00:06:23,632
The eigenvectors of g give
the axes of the ellipse

110
00:06:23,652 --> 00:06:27,292
and the square roots of its
eigenvalues give their lengths.

111
00:06:27,562 --> 00:06:30,284
The long axis points where
the state changes fastest.

112
00:06:30,850 --> 00:06:35,013
In the second paper, Huang builds everything
from the derivative of the projector.

113
00:06:35,283 --> 00:06:40,486
The metric is one half the trace of d P times d P,
and no phase is ever chosen.

114
00:06:41,050 --> 00:06:43,212
Now take a tiny square of momenta instead.

115
00:06:43,482 --> 00:06:47,043
It lands as a tiny parallelogram,
whose area is the

116
00:06:47,063 --> 00:06:50,412
square root of det g,
times delta k x delta k y.

117
00:06:50,682 --> 00:06:53,647
Here det g,
the product of the two eigenvalues

118
00:06:53,667 --> 00:06:56,048
says how much the map magnifies area.

119
00:06:56,616 --> 00:06:58,462
Walk around the square counterclockwise.

120
00:06:58,732 --> 00:07:02,084
Its image is walked either the
same way as most of the map

121
00:07:02,104 --> 00:07:04,177
face up, or the mirror way, flipped.

122
00:07:04,447 --> 00:07:08,434
The Berry curvature is this signed area:
plus twice the area density

123
00:07:08,454 --> 00:07:12,500
where the cell lands face up,
and minus twice where it lands flipped.

124
00:07:13,066 --> 00:07:15,137
Here is a check that this
is the right picture.

125
00:07:15,407 --> 00:07:18,161
The other band sits at the
opposite point of the sphere.

126
00:07:18,431 --> 00:07:21,939
Its parallelogram has the same shape,
so the same metric

127
00:07:21,959 --> 00:07:25,151
but it is flipped,
so the opposite Berry curvature.

128
00:07:25,716 --> 00:07:28,516
Yet the metric of both
bands is that of a sphere

129
00:07:28,536 --> 00:07:31,395
of radius one half,
with Gaussian curvature four.

130
00:07:31,665 --> 00:07:34,450
So the Berry curvature is not
the curvature of the metric.

131
00:07:34,720 --> 00:07:35,673
Keep the two apart.

132
00:07:37,033 --> 00:07:39,735
The derivative of the
projector also tells you how

133
00:07:39,755 --> 00:07:42,565
to travel between states
along the straightest path.

134
00:07:42,835 --> 00:07:45,264
Exponentiate it, and you get a geodesic.

135
00:07:45,534 --> 00:07:48,172
On the Bloch sphere,
geodesics are great circles.

136
00:07:48,733 --> 00:07:52,554
Now carry an arrow around a
triangle made of three geodesics

137
00:07:52,574 --> 00:07:55,882
keeping it as parallel as
the curved surface allows.

138
00:07:56,152 --> 00:07:57,778
When it comes home, it has turned.

139
00:07:58,333 --> 00:08:02,512
The turning angle is the curvature,
four, times the enclosed area

140
00:08:02,532 --> 00:08:05,483
which is just the solid
angle of the triangle.

141
00:08:06,050 --> 00:08:07,891
The quantum states remember the loop too.

142
00:08:08,161 --> 00:08:13,584
Multiply the overlaps around the triangle,
one to two, two to three, three to one.

143
00:08:13,854 --> 00:08:16,762
The phase of this product,
the Bargmann invariant

144
00:08:16,782 --> 00:08:21,124
gives the Berry phase of the triangle,
and it is half of the solid angle.

145
00:08:21,683 --> 00:08:25,890
So the arrow turns by the full solid angle,
while the state picks up half of it.

146
00:08:26,160 --> 00:08:28,788
They are proportional,
but they are not the same thing.

147
00:08:29,350 --> 00:08:31,943
This gives a phase-free way
to compute Berry phases.

148
00:08:32,213 --> 00:08:36,961
Replace a smooth loop by a polygon of
sampled states joined by geodesics.

149
00:08:37,231 --> 00:08:41,936
The polygon's phase converges to the Berry
phase of the loop as you add more points.

150
00:08:43,300 --> 00:08:44,202
Back to the fold.

151
00:08:44,472 --> 00:08:49,483
At every momentum, draw the metric ellipse
from before, with its axes along the

152
00:08:49,503 --> 00:08:54,260
eigenvectors of g and lengths given by
the square roots of the eigenvalues.

153
00:08:54,817 --> 00:08:56,954
Far from the fold the ellipses are fat.

154
00:08:57,224 --> 00:09:01,431
Near the fold one eigenvalue shrinks,
and on the fold it vanishes

155
00:09:01,451 --> 00:09:04,618
det g is zero,
the ellipse collapses to a segment

156
00:09:04,638 --> 00:09:08,260
and one direction of motion
no longer changes the state.

157
00:09:08,530 --> 00:09:12,835
For two bands the Berry curvature
is plus or minus twice root det g

158
00:09:12,855 --> 00:09:16,321
so it vanishes on the fold
and changes sign across it.

159
00:09:16,883 --> 00:09:18,052
That is what a fold is.

160
00:09:18,322 --> 00:09:22,793
Fold a clear sheet printed with arrows,
and the folded part comes down mirrored.

161
00:09:23,063 --> 00:09:27,371
Two different momenta land on the same state,
with opposite orientations.

162
00:09:27,933 --> 00:09:30,764
In our model the fold is
a closed curve around M

163
00:09:30,784 --> 00:09:33,438
where the determinant
of the metric vanishes.

164
00:09:33,708 --> 00:09:35,307
Inside it, the sheet is flipped.

165
00:09:35,867 --> 00:09:38,514
Now count how often each point
of the sphere is covered.

166
00:09:38,784 --> 00:09:42,120
Three momenta, X, Y and M,
all land on the north pole.

167
00:09:42,390 --> 00:09:47,003
Around each of them, a region of the zone
covers the area near the north pole once.

168
00:09:47,567 --> 00:09:50,643
Around X and around Y,
the cover lands face up.

169
00:09:50,913 --> 00:09:53,591
Around M, inside the fold, it lands flipped.

170
00:09:53,861 --> 00:09:58,711
So the region bounded by the image of the fold,
a star with four points

171
00:09:58,731 --> 00:10:04,268
is covered three times, with orientation
signs plus one, plus one, and minus one.

172
00:10:04,538 --> 00:10:07,018
These are orientations, not Chern numbers.

173
00:10:07,583 --> 00:10:10,605
Call each piece of the zone
that is laid down once a sheet.

174
00:10:10,875 --> 00:10:14,908
Count every sheet:
add up the image area of every cell

175
00:10:14,928 --> 00:10:18,961
root det g times delta k x delta k y,
always positive.

176
00:10:19,231 --> 00:10:23,136
That is the quantum volume,
and it comes to one point one nine spheres.

177
00:10:23,700 --> 00:10:27,551
Now count with signs:
face-up cells plus, flipped cells minus

178
00:10:27,571 --> 00:10:30,979
which is the integral of the
Berry curvature over two.

179
00:10:31,249 --> 00:10:34,020
Away from the star every
point is covered once.

180
00:10:34,290 --> 00:10:36,870
Inside it, plus, plus, minus: again once.

181
00:10:37,140 --> 00:10:40,507
So the signed total is exactly
one sphere, the Chern number.

182
00:10:41,067 --> 00:10:44,433
The unsigned total is larger
by twice the area of the star.

183
00:10:44,703 --> 00:10:47,359
That is why, in these units,
the quantum volume

184
00:10:47,379 --> 00:10:49,807
can never be smaller than the Chern number.

185
00:10:51,166 --> 00:10:53,134
The third paper asks a deeper question.

186
00:10:53,404 --> 00:10:56,125
What does the curvature of the
metric know about topology?

187
00:10:56,683 --> 00:11:00,721
For a smooth closed surface,
the Gauss Bonnet theorem says that the

188
00:11:00,741 --> 00:11:04,718
total Gaussian curvature is two pi
times the Euler characteristic.

189
00:11:04,988 --> 00:11:06,320
For a sphere that is four pi

190
00:11:06,590 --> 00:11:07,743
for a torus it is zero.

191
00:11:08,299 --> 00:11:13,214
Away from the folds, our metric is a piece of sphere,
with constant curvature four.

192
00:11:13,484 --> 00:11:15,550
Add it up over all the regular sheets

193
00:11:15,570 --> 00:11:18,875
and you get plus two point three
seven eight, times two pi.

194
00:11:19,145 --> 00:11:19,760
Not zero.

195
00:11:20,316 --> 00:11:21,952
The missing piece lives on the fold.

196
00:11:22,222 --> 00:11:25,803
Treated as a crease,
the fold carries a singular curvature

197
00:11:25,823 --> 00:11:29,963
and it contributes minus two point
three seven eight, times two pi.

198
00:11:30,233 --> 00:11:34,134
The books balance, and the total is zero,
as it must be for a torus.

199
00:11:34,699 --> 00:11:35,974
Now keep the signs instead.

200
00:11:36,244 --> 00:11:39,264
The flipped sheet subtracts,
three sheets become one

201
00:11:39,284 --> 00:11:43,063
and the signed total curvature is
four pi times the Chern number.

202
00:11:43,633 --> 00:11:47,060
These totals do not depend on
how the metric is normalized.

203
00:11:47,330 --> 00:11:51,359
Huang's papers use a metric twice as large,
with sphere curvature two

204
00:11:51,379 --> 00:11:54,058
and find the same two
point three seven eight.

205
00:11:55,416 --> 00:11:57,587
The third paper ends with an open question.

206
00:11:57,857 --> 00:12:00,917
What replaces this picture when
there are more than two bands?

207
00:12:01,187 --> 00:12:02,852
What follows is our own proposal.

208
00:12:03,122 --> 00:12:05,787
It is not yet published,
and it is marked as such.

209
00:12:06,349 --> 00:12:07,476
Count dimensions first.

210
00:12:07,746 --> 00:12:12,738
A state with N components,
normalized and without its phase, needs two N minus

211
00:12:12,758 --> 00:12:17,878
two real numbers: two for two bands,
the Bloch sphere, and four for three bands.

212
00:12:18,148 --> 00:12:20,973
The zone still maps to a
two-dimensional surface

213
00:12:20,993 --> 00:12:23,343
but now inside a four-dimensional space.

214
00:12:23,899 --> 00:12:28,039
A fold needs one direction of motion
in k to stop changing the state.

215
00:12:28,309 --> 00:12:33,022
With two extra dimensions to move into,
a generic surface never has to do that.

216
00:12:33,292 --> 00:12:34,880
The simplest fold shows why.

217
00:12:35,150 --> 00:12:40,333
Here s and t go to s and t squared,
so t and minus t land on the same point.

218
00:12:40,899 --> 00:12:44,185
A third orbital gives the state
a new direction to move in.

219
00:12:44,455 --> 00:12:47,580
Add a small component along it,
epsilon times t

220
00:12:47,600 --> 00:12:52,264
and the two sides no longer meet:
the crease opens into a smooth bend.

221
00:12:52,534 --> 00:12:56,760
In our three-band model,
det g stays positive everywhere we computed it.

222
00:12:57,316 --> 00:12:59,398
Without folds there is no sign to flip.

223
00:12:59,668 --> 00:13:01,086
What replaces it is an angle.

224
00:13:01,356 --> 00:13:06,661
The Berry curvature can never exceed twice
root det g, so define cos theta K as

225
00:13:06,681 --> 00:13:11,850
the Berry curvature divided by this
largest possible value: the Kähler angle.

226
00:13:12,120 --> 00:13:14,629
For two bands it is
always plus or minus one.

227
00:13:14,899 --> 00:13:18,010
For three bands it varies smoothly,
and the shading shows it.

228
00:13:18,566 --> 00:13:20,515
Where it reaches plus or minus one

229
00:13:20,535 --> 00:13:23,700
the surface locally looks
like a two-band sphere again.

230
00:13:23,970 --> 00:13:25,204
These are complex points.

231
00:13:25,474 --> 00:13:28,856
Around each one,
an associated phase winds once

232
00:13:28,876 --> 00:13:32,330
like a vortex,
with index plus one or minus one.

233
00:13:32,600 --> 00:13:37,567
Topology fixes the sum of the indices to
three times the size of the Chern number.

234
00:13:37,837 --> 00:13:40,066
Our example has five complex points

235
00:13:40,086 --> 00:13:44,050
four with plus one and one with minus one,
adding up to three.

236
00:13:44,616 --> 00:13:46,898
And since this surface no longer folds

237
00:13:46,918 --> 00:13:49,804
the ordinary Gauss Bonnet
theorem applies again.

238
00:13:50,074 --> 00:13:54,512
The total Gaussian curvature vanishes,
and it no longer carries the Chern number.

239
00:13:55,866 --> 00:13:57,512
Let's put the three papers together.

240
00:13:57,782 --> 00:13:59,890
The first compares states at a distance

241
00:13:59,910 --> 00:14:02,672
and rebuilds their shape
from a table of distances.

242
00:14:03,233 --> 00:14:06,774
The second differentiates the projector,
giving the metric

243
00:14:06,794 --> 00:14:09,721
the Berry curvature,
geodesics and Berry phases.

244
00:14:09,991 --> 00:14:14,664
The same toolbox gives Wilson loops,
winding numbers and the Z two index.

245
00:14:15,233 --> 00:14:19,280
The third integrates curvature carefully,
folds and signs included

246
00:14:19,300 --> 00:14:22,052
and gets the Chern number
back from geometry.

247
00:14:22,616 --> 00:14:26,951
All of it starts from one object,
the projector, with no phase to fix

248
00:14:26,971 --> 00:14:32,378
and it all lives in one picture:
a torus pressed onto a sphere, folding where it must.

249
00:14:32,648 --> 00:14:33,507
Thanks for watching.
